**Why I'm deviating:** The ticket says "make the callout right from the trigger: one request per new account." But Apex **cannot make a synchronous HTTP callout from a trigger** — you get `CalloutException: Callout from triggers are currently not supported`. Callouts from a trigger context must be asynchronous. Also, Apex has a hard governor limit of **100 synchronous callouts** per transaction; the marketplace integration inserts up to 200 accounts per transaction, so "one request per account" synchronously would hit the limit even if callouts from triggers were allowed.

So I make the callouts from an **`@future(callout=true)` method**, which runs in a separate async context where callouts are permitted. I preserve the "one request per account so the ERP team can trace every account on its own" requirement by looping and sending a separate POST per account (each body is still a valid JSON array, here with a single element, matching the documented contract). The future method's limit is 50 callouts per invocation, so I enqueue the work in chunks of 50 — this keeps the single-record and batch-of-200 cases both working.

